# IPD Patient Flow

## Purpose
IPD owns the inpatient admission record, ward movement, service routing, and discharge readiness. Keep every inpatient action attached to one admission and treat overlays as extensions of that record.

## Stage Sequence
- `ADMITTED_PENDING_BED`: admission exists, bed not assigned.
- `ADMITTED_IN_BED`: patient is admitted and assigned to a ward bed.
- `TRANSFER_REQUESTED` / `TRANSFER_IN_PROGRESS`: internal move is pending or underway.
- `DISCHARGE_PLANNED`: discharge work has started.
- `DISCHARGED`: encounter is complete.
- ICU and theater overlays may appear during the same admission, but they must not create parallel records.

## Handoffs
- Admission desk owns registration, source verification, and bed request coordination.
- Bed manager owns allocation, transfer completion, and bed release.
- Ward nurse owns admission receive, observations, and nursing clearance support.
- Doctor owns assessment, orders, transfer decisions, and discharge planning.
- Billing and pharmacy own clearance checks only; they must not advance clinical stages.

## Hard Invariants
- Must keep the IPD encounter as the central inpatient record.
- Must not duplicate OPD, emergency, ICU, or theater encounters.
- Must not mark discharge complete until clearance steps finish.
- Must not hide pending results, open alerts, or transfer state from the admission view.

## Allowed Critical Actions
- Start ICU stay only when the admission is eligible and the ICU bed is assigned.
- Use `clinical_actions` for inpatient orders so lab, radiology, and pharmacy receive one source of truth.
- Show discharge readiness early, but keep billing, pharmacy, and nursing clearance separate.

